From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nfqueue: activate a batch's direct flows together, and arm
 only in time

The commit pump arms a batch of direct flows under one backend
acquisition, then activated them one at a time: each flow accepted its
held originals, took the backend write lock again and updated its
conn state in a blocking section of its own. A batch of N direct flows
thus waited N times behind whatever held the backend meanwhile (the
janitor's scans hold it for reading), and the pump took no new commits
until the last activation. Under a sustained arrival of new direct UDP
flows, requests aged in the queue, the next batch was armed close to
its three-second hold, and an armed flow that missed it ended the
process ("DirectArmed flow lost its initializer before activation").
On an emulated NanoPi R4S at 200 new flows a second, a batch of 256
flows took 2.6 s to activate one by one, its oldest request already
3 s old; honk exited within minutes.

Activate the batch's armed flows together: accept each flow's held
originals off the backend lock, oldest first, then activate every flow
whose originals are out under one backend acquisition and one blocking
section; a flow whose originals arrived meanwhile accepts them and goes
round again, as before. Arm -> originals -> Activate stays in FIFO
order, and a failure after arming stays fatal.

An armed flow that misses its hold is fatal by design, so arm a flow
only with the time left for its activation, which comes after that of
the flows armed before it in the batch: its deadline less a 300 ms
margin and the activation work of those flows and its own, at a moving
average per flow of the work without the waits for the backend (a
stall must not inflate it), and never more than half the hold, so that
a flow fresh from the queue is always armed and the average stays
current rather than locking every direct flow out. A later flow expires
unarmed, without conn mutation, as a late request already does; its
originals are dropped and its client retries. Overload sheds new flows
instead of ending the process.

Tests: direct flows decided together, while a scanner holds the backend
for reading 150 ms at a time, are all activated (unpatched: the later
ones fail with ArmedInProgress); a flow without time for its activation
expires unarmed, nothing armed and nothing fatal (unpatched: armed);
a flow fresh from the queue is armed however long activations took;
a batch's first armed flow reserves its own activation only and a
later one those before it too; and the reserve follows the recorded
work, up to its bound. The design
document describes both.

